    <!-- SECURITY-COPY: tier G -->
    <string name="files_edit_metadata_present_note">This image may still carry location and camera data.</string>
    <!-- Shown only when MetadataProbe read the image's bytes and found something Delete all
         metadata would remove (2026-10-09). It may say the metadata is there, because it was
         measured; it may not say what it holds, because the probe does not parse it — a comment
         segment and a GPS tag count alike. "can include" is the whole of the claim about content. -->
    <!-- SECURITY-COPY: tier G -->
    <string name="files_edit_metadata_found_note">This image carries metadata, which can include where and when it was taken.</string>
    <!-- "(GPS & EXIF)" was dropped 2026-10-09 at the principal's direction: jargon, and the cleanup
         removes more than those two. -->
    <string name="files_edit_metadata_removed">Metadata removed.</string>
    <!-- Found 2026-09-09. Unlike files_edit_metadata_present_note above, this is
         NOT hedged: PhotoPickerImport.stageFile never runs ExifStripper (or anything else) on a
         video, so unlike an image's cleanup-is-opt-in ambiguity, whether a video's metadata is
         still there is not in question — it always is. Asserts a security property (what the app
         does, and does not, do to stored data), so it is tier G and is tagged as one directly
         below, with its register entry and per-language anchors in TierGClaimsTest.

         This comment previously explained, in prose, that the string was not yet tagged — and
         spelled the marker out to say so. That broke both guards at once: the register test
         matches the marker anywhere inside a comment body, so the key read as tagged and
         unregistered, while the strict extractor could not parse a marker buried in a sentence
         and reported it dark. A tier marker is never prose; it is its own comment and nothing
         else, which is the project rule, and this is what breaking it looks like. -->
    <!-- SECURITY-COPY: tier G -->
